WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.729 --> 00:00:07.509
There have been some genuinely bad numbers
for Donald Trump since the Ukraine fiasco

00:00:07.509 --> 00:00:13.259
started, but we may have gotten the worst
one yet yesterday, and it is that North Carolina

00:00:13.259 --> 00:00:18.779
Republicans are bailing on Trump since the
Ukraine scandal and the impeachment inquiry

00:00:18.779 --> 00:00:24.939
started understand that in the last nine of
10 presidential elections, North Carolina

00:00:24.939 --> 00:00:27.449
has been strongly Republican.

00:00:27.449 --> 00:00:34.570
There is a new Meredith poll were Trump's
North Carolina approval is down to 39.9% now

00:00:34.570 --> 00:00:39.730
you might hear me say 39.9% and you would
correctly say, David, that doesn't sound that

00:00:39.730 --> 00:00:46.100
low because nationally Donald Trump has been
sort of hovering between 37 and 43 for his

00:00:46.100 --> 00:00:47.430
entire presidency.

00:00:47.430 --> 00:00:53.039
39.9 doesn't seem like a big deal, but understand
that this is specifically North Carolina.

00:00:53.039 --> 00:01:01.040
Trump's Republican approval in North Carolina
is down by more than 13% from 86 to 74 not

00:01:01.040 --> 00:01:02.429
13 percentage points.

00:01:02.429 --> 00:01:07.910
It's down 12 percentage points, which is 13%
please don't email me about the math confusion

00:01:07.910 --> 00:01:09.340
that sometimes happens.

00:01:09.340 --> 00:01:14.140
North Carolina is the prototypical center,
right?

00:01:14.140 --> 00:01:20.610
But solidly Republican state, which could
be a bellwether in 2020 you go back to 1980

00:01:20.610 --> 00:01:27.810
North Carolina has gone to the Republican
in the presidential every single time, except

00:01:27.810 --> 00:01:31.380
in 2008 when Barack Obama won it.

00:01:31.380 --> 00:01:32.750
It was very close.

00:01:32.750 --> 00:01:38.000
In 2016 Trump defeated Hillary in North Carolina
by only three and a half percentage points

00:01:38.000 --> 00:01:44.060
in 2016 there already wasn't much of a margin
for error for Trump and North Carolina in

00:01:44.060 --> 00:01:50.460
2016 so when you see his support among Republicans
dropping in the state and driving down the

00:01:50.460 --> 00:01:55.060
overall approval rating for Trump in North
Carolina, you quickly realize that if these

00:01:55.060 --> 00:01:59.400
numbers don't turn around, it does not bode
particularly well for Donald Trump.

00:01:59.400 --> 00:02:05.520
So here's my outlook for the 2020 electorial
college because we often dig into the polls

00:02:05.520 --> 00:02:08.759
and we don't necessarily zoom out and look
at what it might mean.

00:02:08.759 --> 00:02:09.869
Bigger picture.

00:02:09.869 --> 00:02:17.549
Donald Trump has been incorrectly touting
that in 2016 he had some massive records setting

00:02:17.549 --> 00:02:23.310
electorial college when he didn't, but Trump
and some other acolytes are starting to talk

00:02:23.310 --> 00:02:24.379
about, Hey, you know what?

00:02:24.379 --> 00:02:31.609
We might win 40 we might win 42 States or
more in 2020 now it sounds crazy and it probably

00:02:31.609 --> 00:02:33.840
is, but there's another side to that coin.

00:02:33.840 --> 00:02:38.760
If you look back at 2016 Trump won, I believe
it's 30 States.

00:02:38.760 --> 00:02:44.750
Lots of those States were razor thin margins
and Trump still lost the popular vote by almost

00:02:44.750 --> 00:02:46.050
3 million votes.

00:02:46.050 --> 00:02:49.030
Pennsylvania was the 0.7% margin.

00:02:49.030 --> 00:02:52.760
A Florida was a 1.2% margin.

00:02:52.760 --> 00:02:56.129
Michigan was a 0.3% margin.

00:02:56.129 --> 00:02:59.249
Wisconsin was a 0.7% margin.

00:02:59.249 --> 00:03:03.349
North Carolina, as I told you, was about three
and a half percentage points.

00:03:03.349 --> 00:03:11.590
This could go the exact opposite way in 2020
and the Delta, meaning the shift from 2016

00:03:11.590 --> 00:03:15.129
need only be very small for that to happen.

00:03:15.129 --> 00:03:19.409
A few percentage point shift in approval away
from Trump.

00:03:19.409 --> 00:03:24.879
Enough, a handful or even two or three of
these key States such that some Republicans

00:03:24.879 --> 00:03:28.629
decide to vote third party, some Republicans
vote for a Democrat.

00:03:28.629 --> 00:03:30.830
You don't even need that many to do that.

00:03:30.830 --> 00:03:33.739
Some Republicans choose to just stay home.

00:03:33.739 --> 00:03:38.769
That could flip three, four, or five States
easily as a starting point.

00:03:38.769 --> 00:03:41.879
But once again, we can't control that.

00:03:41.879 --> 00:03:47.319
We need to work on what can we control, which
is our turnout.

00:03:47.319 --> 00:03:52.209
If Republicans help us along because they
either choose to stay home or some we'll vote

00:03:52.209 --> 00:03:56.499
for a Democrat, uh, or some of them will vote
third party all the better.

00:03:56.499 --> 00:03:58.219
It just helps the numbers.

00:03:58.219 --> 00:04:02.700
But when you see these numbers in North Carolina
and you go back to 2016 and you look at the

00:04:02.700 --> 00:04:08.590
razor thin margins in some States, if Donald
Trump cannot maintain or regain some of this

00:04:08.590 --> 00:04:12.560
Republican approval in these key States, it
is trouble for him.

00:04:12.560 --> 00:04:15.810
But it is only trouble if we get out and vote.

00:04:15.810 --> 00:04:22.150
And I apologize, I know I'm a broken record
on this, but all of this analysis does not

00:04:22.150 --> 00:04:26.000
matter if our side doesn't come out and vote.

00:04:26.000 --> 00:04:30.310
That's just the math of it and that's why
it's so important to understand that even

00:04:30.310 --> 00:04:34.840
with low approval rating, if we don't vote,
Donald Trump can still get himself reelected.

